---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";

import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import release from "../../data/firmware.json";
import { boards, familyOf, makers } from "../../data/boards.ts";
import { boardsOf } from "../../lib/boards.ts";
import { parseSums } from "../../lib/flash/images.ts";

const fw = "https://github.com/ternmesh/firmware";
const notes = `${fw}/releases/tag/v${release.version}`;

// What the pinned release carries, as tools/firmware.ts left its checksums.
const sums = parseSums(readFileSync(join(process.cwd(), "public", "firmware", "SHA256SUMS"), "utf8"));
const released = boardsOf(release.version, sums);

const running = boards.filter((b) => b.status === "runs");
---

<Docs
    title="Hardware"
    description="Every board Tern's firmware is built for, which run today, and how each is flashed."
>
    <h1>Hardware</h1>
    <p class="lede">
        Tern runs on small LoRa boards of the kind Meshtastic and MeshCore use: {boards.length} of them, from Heltec,
        LilyGo, RAKwireless and Seeed, and <a href="/flash">the flash page</a> offers every one. So far{" "}
        {running.length === 1 ? "one has" : `${running.length} have`} been checked on hardware:{" "}
        <strong>{running.map((b) => b.title).join(", ")}</strong>. The rest are built from their makers' documents
        and wait for someone to try one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="choosing">Choosing a board</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>The surest: a Heltec WiFi LoRa 32 V3.</strong> It is the board Tern is checked on, and it costs
            little. Any other in the table works the same way if its pins are right, and trying one is how it gets
            checked.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The right band.</strong> Every region Tern has is in 863 to 928 MHz: buy that version, not the
            433 or 470 MHz one.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>An antenna, fitted before it is powered.</strong> The one sold with the board is fine to start.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A screen and a button</strong> to see and use the node without a phone. A board without one works,
            with an app.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A battery</strong> if it is to be carried: most of these boards take a single 3.7 V lithium cell
            and charge it over USB. An nRF52840 board runs for days on a cell where an ESP32 runs for hours.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="boards">Every board</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Checked</strong> means someone has flashed a release onto one and checked it: the radio, the screen,
        the battery, the button and a message both ways. <strong>Not yet checked</strong> means the firmware is built
        for it on every change, from its maker's documents, and the release has its images, but nobody has yet said
        it works. It may well; if you try one, <a href={`${fw}/issues`}>say how it went</a>, and it becomes checked.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
        <table class="boards">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Board</th>
                    <th>Chip, radio</th>
                    <th>Status</th>
                    <th>v{release.version}</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                {
                    boards.map((b) => (
                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                {b.title}
                                <br />
                                <span class="muted small">
                                    <a href={b.buy ?? makers[b.maker]}>{b.buy ? `Buy from ${b.maker}` : b.maker}</a>
                                    {!b.buy && <> · no longer made</>}
                                    {b.note && <> · {b.note}</>}
                                </span>
                            </td>
                            <td data-label="Chip">
                                {b.chip}
                                <br />
                                <span class="muted small">{b.radio}</span>
                            </td>
                            <td data-label="Status">
                                {b.status === "runs" ? <strong>Checked</strong> : "Not yet checked"}
                            </td>
                            <td data-label={`v${release.version}`}>
                                {released.has(b.name) ? (
                                    <a href={`/flash?board=${b.name}`}>
                                        {familyOf(b) === "uf2" ? "Download .uf2" : "Flash over USB"}
                                    </a>
                                ) : (
                                    "Not in this release"
                                )}
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    ))
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small">
        This follows the firmware's <a href={`${fw}/blob/main/docs/boards.md`}>board list</a>, which also says what
        each board in waiting is waiting on.
    </p>

    <h2 id="flashing">Flashing each kind</h2>
    <h3>ESP32 boards: from this site, over USB</h3>
    <p>
        <a href="/flash">The flash page</a> writes the release over USB from Chrome or Edge, with nothing to install,
        and can update a board that runs Tern already without losing its address. After that, the phone apps update it
        over Bluetooth. Every image is also in <a href={notes}>the release</a>, to write with Espressif's tools.
    </p>
    <h3>nRF52840 boards: copy a file</h3>
    <p>
        <a href="/flash">The flash page</a> gives you the board's <code>.uf2</code> for your region. Plug the board in
        and press its reset button twice, quickly: it shows up as a USB drive. Copy the file onto it; the board writes
        it and restarts. Its bootloader stays, so going back to other firmware is copying another file on. These
        boards are not yet updated over Bluetooth: copy the next release on the same way.
    </p>
    <h3>A board the release has no image for</h3>
    <p>
        Every change to the firmware is built for every board, and each build's images are kept with it on GitHub,
        under <em>Artifacts</em>. Or build it yourself: the{" "}
        <a href={`${fw}/tree/main/ports/esp32`}>ESP32</a> and <a href={`${fw}/tree/main/ports/nrf52`}>nRF52840</a> ports
        each say how.
    </p>

    <h2 id="next">What comes next</h2>
    <p>
        Most boards Meshtastic and MeshCore run on fall into a few families of chip and radio, and the firmware is
        brought up a family at a time: more ESP32-S3 and nRF52840 boards, LR1110 and LR1121 radios (driven, waiting
        for a board), then the RP2040, the STM32WL and a Raspberry Pi with a LoRa hat. A board's pins come only from
        its maker's own documents, so some wait on a maker to publish a detail. The firmware's{" "}
        <a href={`${fw}/blob/main/docs/boards.md#every-board-meshtastic-and-meshcore-run-on`}>board list</a> keeps the
        plan.
    </p>
</Docs>

<style>
    .small {
        font-size: 0.875rem;
    }
    /* On a phone each board is a card: its name, then a line for each of the other columns. */
    @media (max-width: 40rem) {
        .boards thead {
            display: none;
        }
        .boards,
        .boards tbody,
        .boards tr,
        .boards td {
            display: block;
        }
        .boards tr {
            padding: 10px 0;
            border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
        }
        .boards td {
            border: 0;
            padding: 2px 0;
        }
        .boards td[data-label] {
            display: flex;
            gap: 8px;
        }
        .boards td[data-label]::before {
            content: attr(data-label);
            flex: 0 0 7.5rem;
            color: var(--muted);
        }
        .boards td[data-label] br {
            display: none;
        }
        .boards td[data-label] .small {
            margin-left: 6px;
        }
    }
</style>
